W32A-12 — WS2_32 surface probe, from the pinned PuTTY 0.85 (Win64) binaries
==========================================================================
Base commit:
Tooling:     x86_64-w64-mingw32-objdump -p / strings ; sha256sum

Discovery vehicle
-----------------
The plan (§2.6) requires this phase to discover WS2_32's surface with a
logging run "since static tables cannot show it": WS2_32 is not in either
binary's import directory — PuTTY resolves it at run time with
LoadLibrary("WS2_32")+GetProcAddress, so the static app-import ledger
(w32/app_ledger/putty-0.85.imports) carries ZERO ws2_32 rows.  The names
therefore have to be read out of the binary itself.

The OS build must never require downloading a binary (w32/LICENSING.md,
D8 rule), so the two PuTTY executables are NOT vendored in git.  They are
fetched into build/w32bins/ (git-ignored and exempt from the provenance
MZ scan) purely as a local verification aid.  The fetch + measurement is
fully reproducible:

    URL   https://the.earth.li/~sgtatham/putty/0.85/w64/putty.exe
    URL   https://the.earth.li/~sgtatham/putty/0.85/w64/plink.exe
    putty.exe  size 1706136  sha256 d01fdb5aae8f112526040a39b0bfb9e27d813003178645e65f8d1cfdb2a26c87
    plink.exe  size 1036448  sha256 969f36879d5716aa1a9811f43a6a6510e8f08372dbeb9695b810b9c776f39c75

    (putty.exe's sha256 + size match the committed static ledger
     w32/app_ledger/putty-0.85.imports header exactly.)

Method — the WS2_32 names each binary passes to GetProcAddress are the
NUL-terminated ASCII symbol strings in .rdata.  A name that is a suffix of
a longer string (e.g. "socket" inside "closesocket", "connect") is stored
as a pointer into that string by the linker's string-pool merge, so the
scan accepts any "name\0" whose preceding byte is a non-identifier char:

    strings -n 3 putty.exe        # human view of the .rdata pool
    # then: for each winsock name, test  name\0  at a string boundary

Static import directories (objdump -p) — WS2_32 absent from both, proving
the dynamic-load path:
    putty.exe : ADVAPI32 COMDLG32 GDI32 IMM32 KERNEL32 SHELL32 USER32 ole32
    plink.exe : KERNEL32 USER32 ADVAPI32

